{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/854303-troupe-play-for-agencies-in-dtd","id":854303,"title":"Troupe play for agencies in DtD","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-03-27T18:47:02-07:00","id":854303,"title":"Troupe play for agencies in DtD","content":"Hey folks if you've seen the Dev Notes for The Pack, you've seen what they're offering for Werewolf packs. In addition to giving me the hope that they'll do a book just like it, but for demonic agencies, (which we can add to the already exhaustive wishlist we've assembled for future DtD material) I was intrigued by the inclusion of troupe play for Uratha packs, which makes sense given the emphasis of pack as large social organizations including Uratha, wolfbloods, and human allies/collaborators.<br /> <br /> And I realized, &quot;damn, that makes even more sense for Demon.&quot;<br /> <br /> One of the setting details that's always been difficult to implement in DtD games (at least the one I've run) is the distrust, the sense that virtually anyone can be an infiltrator of a dozen different varieties (Demons, sleepers, simularca, cultists, etc.) and that another demon is <i>especially</i> untrustworthy. The in-game characters may be able to lie perfectly, but the players can't, a betrayal can lead to any number of hurt feelings. If you put the characters in an agency, and have the agency get infiltrated/compromised, the players wonder &quot;why are we bothering, if this is going to blow up in our faces?&quot;<br /> <br /> Give a multitude of diverse characters, characters that the players are <i>invested</i> in, and want to interact with... and you have a recipe for real intrigue. You now have an agency full of assets that you have to run, disengaging is not an option.<br /> <br /> Imagine telling your players &quot;In addition to your main demon character, each of you create 4 simple characters, each one of them has one or two unique assets (membership in law enforcement, deep cover in Deva, psychic powers, wealth, etc) that benefits the agency, choose one of them, in secrete, to be a security risk. Not necessarily a traitor, but someone the characters cannot trust 100%&quot; The other players have a wealth of allies to help them... but always at a risk of getting burned.<br /> <br /> Take it one step further and create a trust matrix. Each one of the creates 4 simple characters, then present them to the rest of the group, the rest of the group, secretly and individually rates each NPC, giving each a trust point, or labeling them a security risk. The more trust points Ed, the agency's legal council, might have a number of contacts for getting the players out of trouble... but he also might be embezzling funds or have a recreational drug habit that might attract the SEC, the FBI, etc. to the agency's door.<br /> <br /> Players will (ideally) be invested in the characters they created, and will resent the suggestion that their characters are untrustworthy... while not knowing that the people they're defending are untrustworthy, think the relationship between Mr. White and Mr. Orange in <i>Reservoir Dogs</i>.<br /> <br /> Take it another step further: everyone assigns trust points individually and makes up a certain number of security risk tags, which are shuffled then assigned at random by the storyteller, including a &quot;Trust +1&quot; card or two to keep players guessing, and give the possibility that maybe they suspect an innocent person. Susan might be a sleeper agent, or she might have SVR connections, or she might be innocent, but you'll never know unless you investigate and run the risk of having her turn on the agency.<br /> <br /> In play, whomsoever is running an agency character won't know whether or not they character they're running is trust worthy, though the storyteller will slip them some notes with mysterious instructions or give them secret goals to rachett up paranoia (e.g. &quot;try to get Ms. Child to say the phrase 'fairy tale' without having her realize what you're doing.&quot;) When the time comes for security risk to come to the fore, the storyteller can take a player aside an brief them of how character is going to betray or compromise the agency.<br /> <br /> Thoughts? Suggested improvements?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2233-agentwestmer","name":"Agentwestmer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2233_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-28T06:01:30-07:00","id":854433,"title":"","content":"I think more information could easily be had on Unchained organizations, which very much includes all the non-Demon personnel that such Agencies require. Stigmatics, normal Humans (&quot;briefed&quot; or ignorant), Cryptids, and whatever else the Unchained can enlist. <br /> <br /> Regarding your Paranoia suggestion, as a general game type, I love it. Might be hard to manage very often though, and is highly dependent upon the play group. You could always have the Storyteller generate characters too. Or even make such &quot;new hires&quot; a collaborative effort - The Storyteller gives some details, and each player goes around filling in their own. Or submits their own to the ST, who chooses which to use (So if Player A states that Fred in HR has a terminally ill sister, and Player B states that he is a plant by Deva, the Storyteller chooses which to use, if not both). Each player has something they may suspect, but don't outright know to be true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}